---
/**
 * The cohort page.
 *
 * WHERE THE WORDS COME FROM
 *
 * Every line of prose below is from the copy Team Alchemy delivered on
 * 10 September — docs/Website Rebuild 10-09-2026/cohort-page.md — reproduced
 * rather than paraphrased. It is approved copy about a real person's real
 * offer, and "improving" a sentence here is how a claim nobody checked reaches
 * a page. If a line reads awkwardly, that is a note for the copy round, not an
 * edit to make in a component.
 *
 * The section order is the brief's own module table, widened by the sections
 * restored on 15 September. It is not arbitrary: outcome, fit, example, what a
 * review changes, outcomes, the transformation, the work, the modules,
 * process, Sunil, what you leave with, live experience, commitment, questions,
 * a call, application. Somebody arrives wondering whether this is for them, and
 * each section answers the next question they have.
 *
 * THE PRINTED NUMBERS RUN 00 TO 15, IN DOCUMENT ORDER, WITH NO GAPS. They were
 * 00, 01, 02, 04, 03, 05 with five sections unnumbered, because the restored
 * sections kept the numbers they carried on the pre-rebuild page and were
 * interleaved with rebuild sections that carried none. If you add or move a
 * section, renumber the whole run. A reader uses these to tell how far through
 * they are, and a sequence that skips or repeats tells them the wrong thing.
 *
 * THE HERO PORTRAIT, RESTORED 15 September 2026
 *
 * The 10 September rebuild put a reserved video slot (VSL) here instead of a
 * portrait, because the brief's brief was "before footage is available, keep
 * the written page complete and omit the player." Sunil asked for the earlier
 * design back — portrait, proof bar, the fuller section set below — on
 * 15 September, after reviewing the pre-rebuild page directly
 * (git: 845ca99^:src/components/ProgramPage.astro). If the VSL is recorded
 * later, it is a slot to reintroduce beside this, not a reason to remove it
 * again; nothing here currently plays a video.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT ON THIS PAGE, AND WHY
 *
 *   * No "seats remaining", and no countdown. The price, the dates and the
 *     week count ARE here (D1, 14 September 2026) and come from facts.ts and
 *     regions.ts; scarcity is the thing that stays off, because eight capped
 *     seats is a fact and "only two left" is a sales tactic.
 *   * No unsourced audience/product-scale claims. The pre-rebuild page's stat
 *     band and prose (100M+ users served, 150 engineers led, ~31 billion
 *     weekly executions, 300+ products modernised) had no provenance in this
 *     repository — an 11 September QA finding removed that class of claim
 *     from every public surface (docs/cohort-pipeline/RESUME.md) and it does
 *     NOT come back with the rest of the restored design.
 *   * The mentoring/coaching/training counts (100+ mentored, 100+ coached,
 *     ~100 trained) are a DIFFERENT claim — flagged in the same finding as
 *     awaiting a decision, not as unsourced — and were approved for
 *     republication on 15 September 2026. They appear in "Live experience".
 *   * No response-time promise. One business day to acknowledge is a proposal
 *     "subject to capacity agreement" in the operating guide, and the guide
 *     says in terms: do not place it in public copy as a guarantee.
 */
import RouteForm from './RouteForm.astro';
import BookNowLink from '../site/BookNowLink.astro';
import BookingWidget from '../BookingWidget.astro';
import { cohort } from '../../data/facts';
import type { Region } from '../../data/regions';
import {
  EXPLORES,
  QUESTIONS,
  PERSONAS,
  TRANSFORMATION,
  MODULES,
  LEAVE_WITH,
  LIVE_EXPERIENCE,
} from '../../data/cohort-copy';

/**
 * D1 was answered on 14 September 2026: the figures are published. A region is
 * passed in because the rate is per region and only some are published — see
 * `publicPrice` in regions.ts. The page and the JSON-LD Offer node in
 * index.astro read the same region, so a visitor and a crawler cannot be shown
 * different numbers.
 */
interface Props {
  region?: Region | null;
}

const { region = null } = Astro.props;
const hasPrice = Boolean(region?.publicPrice);

---

{/* ── 00 · Hero ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section class="hero" id="top" style="border-top:0">
  <div class="wrap hero-grid">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="num">00</span> An application-only program in agentic &amp; systems architecture{region ? ` · ${region.label}` : ''}</span>
      <h1>Build systems<br />that <em>outlive</em><br />their makers.</h1>
      <p class="credential">
        I'm <strong>Sunil Mathew</strong>. I have spent 26 years building and leading
        engineering at <strong>Google</strong>, <strong>Amazon</strong>, and <strong>Walmart</strong>.
        Here I teach the craft of agentic and systems architecture, live, to a small cohort.
      </p>

      {/* Apply is primary; Book now is the secondary path, right beside it —
          the same pairing the top nav and the sticky bar carry on every other
          page. Book now opens section 14 below, which is this site's own
          booking widget reading real availability, so there is nothing to
          configure and nothing that can render as a dead control. There is no
          third "ask a question" link here any more — that was a separate
          enquiry form, and it is now one branching application flow in
          section 15. */}
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#apply">Apply for the {cohort.startsOn} cohort&nbsp;<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <BookNowLink placement="hero" onCohortPage />
      </div>

      <p class="hero-note">
        {region ? `Inaugural ${region.label} cohort` : 'Inaugural cohort'} · starts {cohort.startsOn} · {cohort.seats} seats, capped · applications reviewed personally
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="portrait">
      <div class="frame">
        <img src="/sunil-profile.jpg" alt="Sunil Mathew" />
        <div class="warm"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tag"><b>Sunil Mathew</b>Engineering leader · Google, Amazon, Walmart</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── Proof bar ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<div class="proofbar">
  <div class="wrap inner">
    <span class="label">26 years, shipped at</span>
    <span class="logo">Google</span>
    <span class="logo">Amazon</span>
    <span class="logo">Walmart</span>
    <span class="logo">Consulting</span>
  </div>
</div>

{/* ── 01 · Audience fit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section class="wrap band" id="fit">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="num">01</span> Who this is for</span>
    <h2>For engineers who've hit the <em>ceiling</em> of what tutorials teach.</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      You can build. What you want now is judgment. That comes from shipping hard
      things and living with the consequences. It is not in a course catalogue. It
      transfers from one person to another.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="cards3">
    {
      PERSONAS.map((p) => (
        <div class="card">
          <div class="k">{p.k}</div>
          <h3>{p.title}</h3>
          <p>{p.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 02 · Practical example ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section class="wrap band" id="example">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">02</span> An illustrative example</p>
    <h2>Follow a decision <em>beyond the demo</em></h2>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="example-body">
      <p class="body-copy">
        Imagine a returns assistant that reads a policy and recommends a refund. Now
        give it a tool that can issue the refund. The system can move money, and its
        design needs to account for that responsibility.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        What evidence permits the action? How does it recognise a repeated request?
        What happens when the policy is unclear or a tool result is uncertain?
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy quiet">
        This illustrative example shows the kind of connection you will practise:
        from a design choice to behaviour you can examine.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* The one structural idea on the page that is a diagram rather than a
        sentence — and it earns it, because the whole argument is that these
        are two different responsibilities that look like one box. */}
    <ol class="boundary" aria-label="Recommendation and action are separate responsibilities">
      <li>
        <span class="b-k">Reads</span>
        <b>The policy</b>
        <span class="b-n">Gathers the evidence it will rely on.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="b-k">Recommends</span>
        <b>A refund</b>
        <span class="b-n">A person can still read this and decide.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="b-line">
        <span class="b-k">The boundary</span>
        <b>What permits the action?</b>
        <span class="b-n">Evidence, limits, repeated requests, an uncertain result.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="b-k">Acts</span>
        <b>Issues the refund</b>
        <span class="b-n">The system moves money. Its own controls, not the prompt’s.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 03 · What a review changes ───────────────────────────────────────────
    Added by the V4 addendum, 11 September. It sits between the example and
    the outcomes deliberately: the returns assistant above shows a design
    QUESTION, and this shows what asking it produces. Without this section the
    page demonstrates the questions and never the consequence of asking them,
    which is the thing being sold.

    It is a second illustrative example and says so twice, in the copy's own
    words. Neither is a claimed incident. */}
<section class="wrap band" id="review">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">03</span> A second illustrative example</p>
    <h2>See what a review <em>can change</em></h2>
  </div>
  <div class="two-up">
    <p class="lead">
      Imagine a checker rejects an agent's result, and the workflow repeats the same
      attempt. A useful review asks what new evidence the next attempt has. If nothing
      changes, the next design question is how to recover or stop.
    </p>
    <p class="body-copy">
      This is an illustrative teaching example. It shows how a question can lead to a
      change you can build and examine. The aim is to develop how you reason, alongside
      what you produce.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 04 · Outcomes and curriculum ───────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section class="wrap band" id="outcomes">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">04</span> What you develop</p>
    <h2>Develop practical <em>design judgment</em></h2>
  </div>
  <div class="two-up">
    <p class="lead">
      Build a working agentic system and connect its behaviour to the architecture
      behind it. Practise explaining why a boundary exists, what evidence supports a
      decision and what you would change next.
    </p>
    <p class="body-copy">
      Develop how you design and critique agentic systems, review a team’s proposal
      and provide useful guidance.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 05 · The transformation ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    Restored 15 September 2026, from the pre-rebuild page. A–E are
    `cohort.outcomes` in facts.ts, already fed to JSON-LD and the Q&A agent;
    F is previously-published copy carried verbatim (see TRANSFORMATION in
    cohort-copy.ts for why it is not treated as an invented figure). */}
<section class="wrap transform" id="transform">
  <div class="sec-head" style="margin-bottom:0">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="num">05</span> The transformation</span>
    <h2>What you'll be able to do.</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      By the end, the work shows up in how you decide. Under real constraints, with
      real stakes.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ol class="tlist">
    {
      TRANSFORMATION.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <span class="n">{item.letter}.</span>
          <div>
            <h4>{item.title}</h4>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <p class="lead" style="margin-top:32px">
    Then carry it forward. The real measure of having learned the craft is being
    able to hand it on.
  </p>
</section>

{/* ── 06 · What the work explores ─────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section class="wrap band" id="curriculum">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">06</span> The work</p>
    <h2>What the work <em>explores</em></h2>
  </div>
  {/* Five areas, not five modules with durations. The brief: "Use validated
      learning areas; no invented module durations." The old page carried four
      modules with week ranges; those weeks are not in the delivered copy and
      are not restated here. */}
  <ol class="tlist explores">
    {
      EXPLORES.map((item, i) => (
        <li>
          <span class="n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <div>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
</section>

{/* ── 07 · Inside the program ────────────────────────────────────────────
    Restored 15 September 2026. The four modules are `cohort.modules` in
    facts.ts; the body copy for each is not invented — it is the description
    the pre-rebuild page carried for the same four modules. */}
<section class="wrap band" id="modules">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="num">07</span> Inside the program</span>
    <h2>A small set of deep things, done <em>properly</em>.</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Six weeks. Live, in a small cohort. Built around your real systems, not toy
      problems.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="modules">
    {
      MODULES.map((m) => (
        <div class="mod">
          <div class="mk"><span class="num">{m.id}</span><span class="wk">{m.weeks}</span></div>
          <h4>{m.title}</h4>
          <p>{m.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 08 · Learning process ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section class="wrap band" id="process">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">08</span> How it runs</p>
    <h2>Build, examine and <em>revise</em></h2>
  </div>
  <div class="two-up">
    <p class="lead">
      The working system gives the discussions something concrete to return to.
      Build an approach, examine what happens and use continuous feedback to
      understand strengths, gaps and follow-up work.
    </p>
    <p class="body-copy">
      Industry-specific practice is the default. Company-specific work needs a
      separate conversation about scope and permission.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 09 · Sunil ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
{/* THE MAKER SECTION, REINSTATED 15 September 2026.
 *
 * This was section 03 of the pre-rebuild page (ProgramPage.astro) and the
 * 10 September rebuild replaced it with the delivered paragraph alone. The
 * paragraph is still here and still first, unedited — it is approved copy.
 * What comes back is the portrait, the scale figures and the narrative.
 *
 * WHAT DID NOT COME BACK, AND WHY. The old section led with a numeric stat
 * band — 100M+ users served, 150 engineers led — and prose carrying ~31
 * billion weekly executions and 300+ products modernised. A QA finding on
 * 11 September removed exactly that class of claim from every public surface,
 * and docs/cohort-pipeline/RESUME.md records it: "the unapproved stat band and
 * product-scale claims were removed rather than inferred from older material".
 * CLAUDE.md's Instructor rule says the same thing. Putting them back here
 * would reverse a fix somebody made on purpose four days ago.
 *
 * So the band carries the four verbs /caio uses after that same cleanup, and
 * the prose keeps the approved employment context: the tenure, the three
 * employers and startups, the current agentic-AI practice. If a dated approval
 * record for the figures exists, the numbers are a one-block edit and the old
 * wording is in git at 845ca99^:src/components/ProgramPage.astro.
 *
 * Third person throughout, because the delivered paragraph is third person and
 * the old section was first. The only "I" on the page is inside the quotation
 * marks, with the signature reading as its attribution.
 */}
<section class="wrap band" id="sunil">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">09</span> Who teaches it</p>
    <h2>Work with <em>Sunil Mathew</em></h2>
  </div>

  <div class="statband">
    <div class="stat"><div class="big">Build</div><div class="cap">Engineering systems</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="big">Lead</div><div class="cap">Guide technical decisions</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="big">Review</div><div class="cap">Examine evidence and risk</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="big">Teach</div><div class="cap">Transfer design judgment</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="story">
    <div class="maker-portrait">
      <div class="mframe"><img src="/sunil-bw.jpg" alt="Sunil Mathew" /></div>
      <div class="mcap">Sunil Mathew · Bengaluru, India</div>
    </div>
    <div>
      {/* The delivered copy, reproduced and not edited. */}
      <p class="lead sunil-copy">
        Sunil brings engineering and leadership experience from Google, Amazon, Walmart
        and startups. His focus is the reasoning behind a system’s design and the
        evidence that helps a team make its next decision. His teaching connects a
        concrete example, a question about the design and a change that can be examined.
      </p>
      <p>
        He has spent twenty-six years building and leading engineering at
        <strong>Google</strong>, <strong>Amazon</strong>, <strong>Walmart</strong> and
        startups. The work was production systems and the teams that run them, where a
        design decision is expensive to undo once it ships.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today he leads <strong>agentic-AI</strong> work: multi-agent orchestration, RAG,
        evals and LLM-Ops. He is also building an agentic-AI product of his own. The
        judgment he teaches here is current practice rather than a memory of one.
      </p>
      <div class="pullquote">“Anyone can show you the agent pattern. I can show you the three times it was the wrong pattern in production, and how I knew before it cost us.”</div>
      <div class="signature quote-attrib">— Sunil Mathew</div>
      <p class="after-quote">
        That is what the 30 live hours are for. You bring a design decision from your own
        work. He shows you how he would examine it, and what evidence would settle it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 10 · What you leave with ───────────────────────────────────────────
    Restored 15 September 2026, verbatim from the pre-rebuild page. No
    figures here — artifacts and access, not metrics — so nothing about it
    was in question. */}
<section class="wrap band" id="takeaways">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="num">10</span> What you leave with</span>
    <h2>What physically <em>leaves the room</em>.</h2>
    <p class="lead">Not just capabilities. Artifacts you keep, and your team inherits.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cards3">
    {
      LEAVE_WITH.map((t) => (
        <div class="card">
          <div class="k">{t.k}</div>
          <h3>{t.title}</h3>
          <p>{t.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 11 · Live experience ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    Restored 15 September 2026, with one deliberate omission — see the
    LIVE_EXPERIENCE comment in cohort-copy.ts for exactly which figures did
    and did not come back, and why. */}
<section class="wrap band inaugural" id="live-experience">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="num">11</span> Live experience</span>
    <h2>I've spent a career growing <em>people</em>, not just systems.</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      The craft only counts if it transfers, and it already has. This is the track
      record I bring into the room.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="cards3">
    {
      LIVE_EXPERIENCE.map((e) => (
        <div class="card">
          <div class="k">{e.k}</div>
          <h3>{e.title}</h3>
          <p>{e.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 12 · Commitment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section class="wrap band" id="commitment">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">12</span> The commitment</p>
    <h2>Make room for <em>the work</em></h2>
  </div>

  <div class="commit">
    <div class="commit-copy">
      <p class="body-copy">
        The programme includes 30 live hours with Sunil, plus independent work.
        Confirm the session schedule, independent-work commitment, attendance support
        and access arrangements before joining.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        The cohort is capped at {cohort.seats} seats. {cohort.enrollment}.
        Applying starts a discussion; admission, payment and attendance confirmation
        are separate steps.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="commit-facts">
      <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Live hours</span><span class="dv">30, with Sunil</span></div>
      <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Independent work</span><span class="dv">Additional; confirmed before joining</span></div>
      <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Cohort size</span><span class="dv">{cohort.seats} seats, capped</span></div>
      <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Length</span><span class="dv">{cohort.weeks} weeks, live</span></div>
      <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Starts</span><span class="dv">{cohort.startsOn}</span></div>
      <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Admission</span><span class="dv">{cohort.admission}</span></div>
      <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Certificate</span><span class="dv">None</span></div>
      <div class="detail-row">
        <span class="dk">Payment due</span>
        <span class="dv">{cohort.paymentDue}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="price-card">
    {/* Sentence case, and it has to be built that way rather than left to CSS:
        .price-card .seat is text-transform: none, so a visitor with no region
        resolved read a lowercase "founding seats" where an India visitor read
        "Inaugural India cohort · founding seats". */}
    <div class="seat">{region ? `Inaugural ${region.label} cohort · founding seats` : 'Founding seats'}</div>
    {hasPrice ? (
      <Fragment>
        <div class="amt">
          {region!.standardPrice && <span class="was">{region!.standardPrice}</span>}
          {region!.price} <small>{region!.priceUnit}</small>
        </div>
        <div class="terms">
          {region!.standardPrice
            ? `Founding rate for the first cohort. It rises to ${region!.standardPrice} for the cohorts that follow. Payment is due ${cohort.paymentDue}. Payment plans are available.`
            : `Founding rate for the first cohort. It rises for the cohorts that follow. Payment is due ${cohort.paymentDue}. Payment plans are available.`}
        </div>
      </Fragment>
    ) : (
      <Fragment>
        <div class="amt" style="font-size:36px">Shared on application</div>
        <div class="terms">
          The fee is set per region and is shared on application. The first
          cohort enrols at a founding rate, which rises for the cohorts that
          follow. Payment is due {cohort.paymentDue}. Applying commits you to
          nothing.
        </div>
      </Fragment>
    )}
    <ul>
      <li>Starts {cohort.startsOn} — {cohort.weeks} weeks live, in a founding cohort of {cohort.seats}</li>
      <li>Your real architecture, pressure-tested live with the cohort</li>
      <li>Direct access to Sunil throughout</li>
      <li>Lifetime access to the cohort room</li>
      {region?.hybrid && <li>Bangalore: attend in person or online</li>}
    </ul>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#apply">Apply for a seat&nbsp;<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 13 · FAQs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section class="wrap band" id="questions">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">13</span> Before you apply</p>
    <h2>Questions before <em>joining</em></h2>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    {
      QUESTIONS.map((item) => (
        <details>
          <summary>
            {item.q}
            <span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">
              +
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p>{item.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 14 · Application ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
{/* ONE APPLICATION FLOW, BRANCHING BY WHO IS APPLYING.

    Until this rework, three separate forms sat here — the open-cohort
    application, a team/enterprise enquiry beside it, and a third "Ask about
    the cohort" panel folded under a dropdown. That third form is gone: the
    site's secondary CTA is now Book now (a real appointment — see the hero
    and the top nav), and a written question is one email away, in the
    contact line below. What is left branches on one thing — applying for
    yourself, or arranging learning for a team — because those really are two
    different journeys (forms.ts: an application and an enterprise enquiry
    are different record types, and an enterprise enquiry is never counted as
    an application), while a plain question about fit was never a
    commitment either form is built to capture.

    Only one branch's form is visible at a time (route-choice.ts equivalent
    is the inline script below), so a visitor sees ONE form, not two stacked
    ones — the biggest single cut to how much scrolling the mobile flow
    needs. Each form itself is now progressive (see RouteForm.astro's
    `steps`), so nobody meets all seven-plus fields on one screen either. */}
{/* ── 14 · Book a call ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Where every "Book now" on the site lands.

    It is THIS SITE'S booking widget, the same one /caio and /assessment use,
    against the `cohort-call` meeting type in meetings.ts. Availability is read
    from our own `booking_rules`, so there is no third-party popup to configure
    and no dead control to guard against — the reason the Google Calendar
    scaffold it replaced was invisible until somebody supplied a URL.

    IT SITS ABOVE THE APPLICATION, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. BookingWidget's
    own empty and failure states say "use the form below" — on this page that
    sentence is only true while the Apply section follows it. Moving this
    below the form silently turns that copy into a lie.

    A call is NOT an application. The meeting type's blurb says so, and Apply
    stays the primary CTA in the hero, the nav, the price card and the sticky
    bar. */}
<section class="wrap band" id="book">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">14</span> Talk first</p>
    <h2>Book a call about <em>the cohort</em></h2>
  </div>

  <BookingWidget type="cohort-call" heading="Book a call about the cohort" />
</section>

<section class="wrap band forms" id="apply">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">15</span> Apply</p>
    <h2>Start <em>here</em></h2>
    <p class="lead">
      One flow, however you are arriving at it. Pick the one that matches you, then answer a
      few questions at a time.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="route-choice" role="group" aria-label="What brings you here?">
    <button
      type="button"
      class="route-choice-btn"
      data-route-choice="application"
      aria-pressed="true"
    >
      <span class="rc-title">Apply for the open cohort</span>
      <span class="rc-body">Joining as an individual — self-funded or exploring employer support.</span>
    </button>
    <button
      type="button"
      class="route-choice-btn"
      data-route-choice="enterprise"
      aria-pressed="false"
    >
      <span class="rc-title">Arrange learning for a team</span>
      <span class="rc-body">Scoping an enterprise group programme for your organisation.</span>
    </button>
  </div>

  <div id="apply-panel" class="route-panel" tabindex="-1">
    <RouteForm route="application" id="apply-form" emphasis="primary" heading="Apply for the open cohort" />
  </div>

  <div id="team" class="route-panel" tabindex="-1" hidden>
    <RouteForm route="enterprise" id="enterprise-form" heading="Arrange learning for a team" />
  </div>

  <p class="contact-line">
    Programme enquiries and employer-funding support, and enterprise enquiries:
    <a href="mailto:apply@thelivingcraft.ai">apply@thelivingcraft.ai</a>
  </p>
</section>

{/* Switches which of the two forms above is visible, and runs the arrival
    cue.

    A button pair rather than a <select> or radios, because there are only two
    and each needs room for a description — the same reasoning RouteForm's own
    field choices follow.

    THE CUE. Arriving from any Apply CTA closes both forms, lights BOTH
    buttons, and waits for a choice. It does not open a form by itself: an
    earlier version did that after 900ms, which read as the form simply
    opening on arrival, and picked the individual route on the reader's
    behalf into the bargain. The point is that the team route is seen at all
    — with a form already open, the chooser was something a reader scrolled
    past on the way to the fields. Anyone who scrolls here themselves, with
    no Apply click behind them, gets the individual form open as before; the
    cue is only for people who were sent here by a CTA.

    Three links elsewhere on the site point straight at a branch and must
    skip the cue entirely: #team and #enterprise-form (the contact page, and
    the footer's "Learning for a team") open the team form directly, and
    #apply-form opens the individual one. Somebody who asked for one form
    does not need to be shown that a choice exists. */}
<script>
  (function () {
    const buttons = Array.from(
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-route-choice]'),
    );
    const panels: Record<string, HTMLElement | null> = {
      application: document.getElementById('apply-panel'),
      enterprise: document.getElementById('team'),
    };
    if (buttons.length === 0 || !panels.application || !panels.enterprise) return;

    const select = (branch: string, focus: boolean) => {
      if (!panels[branch]) return;
      buttons.forEach((b) =>
        b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.routeChoice === branch)),
      );
      Object.entries(panels).forEach(([key, panel]) => {
        if (panel) panel.hidden = key !== branch;
      });
      if (focus) panels[branch]!.focus();
    };

    const endCue = () => {
      buttons.forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('is-cueing'));
    };

    /**
     * Highlight both choices and WAIT.
     *
     * There is no timer here on purpose. A previous version opened the
     * individual form by itself after 900ms, which read as the form simply
     * opening on arrival — the highlight was over before anybody registered
     * it, and the choice had been made on the reader's behalf anyway. The
     * highlight now holds until somebody picks, which is the whole point of
     * showing them two things.
     */
    const cue = () => {
      // Both panels closed, so the two buttons are the only thing to look at.
      Object.values(panels).forEach((panel) => {
        if (panel) panel.hidden = true;
      });
      // Nothing is selected while the question is open. Leaving the
      // individual button pressed would answer it for them.
      buttons.forEach((b) => {
        b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', 'false');
        // Restart the animation on a second click, which it will not do on
        // its own while the class is already there.
        b.classList.remove('is-cueing');
        void b.offsetWidth;
        b.classList.add('is-cueing');
      });
    };

    buttons.forEach((b) => {
      b.addEventListener('click', () => {
        // The choice answers the cue, so the cue stops being asked.
        endCue();
        select(b.dataset.routeChoice ?? 'application', true);
      });
    });

    const fromHash = () => {
      if (location.hash === '#team' || location.hash === '#enterprise-form') {
        endCue();
        select('enterprise', false);
      } else if (location.hash === '#apply-form') {
        endCue();
        select('application', false);
      } else if (location.hash === '#apply') {
        cue();
      }
    };
    fromHash();
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', fromHash);

    /* A second click on an Apply CTA fires no hashchange, because the hash is
       already #apply — so the cue would run once and never again. This
       catches the click itself, on this page only; the browser still does the
       scrolling. */
    document.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      const target = event.target as HTMLElement | null;
      const link = target?.closest?.('a[href]') as HTMLAnchorElement | null;
      if (!link) return;
      const url = new URL(link.href, location.href);
      const samePage = url.pathname.replace(/\/+$/, '') === location.pathname.replace(/\/+$/, '');
      if (samePage && url.hash === '#apply') cue();
    });
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* 56px rather than 72. This page is the long one — sixteen sections — and a
     notch off each band takes ~500px out of the scroll without any section
     reading as cramped. The two responsive steps are in global.css, with the
     rest of the site's density pass. */
  .band { padding-block: calc(var(--space-7) + var(--space-2)); }

  /* ── prose ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .two-up {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 0.9fr;
    gap: var(--space-6) var(--space-7);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .body-copy {
    font-size: var(--size-4);
    line-height: var(--lh-normal);
    color: var(--text-body);
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  }
  .body-copy + .body-copy { margin-top: var(--space-4); }
  .body-copy.quiet { color: var(--text-quiet); font-size: var(--size-3); }
  .sunil-copy { max-width: var(--measure-prose); }

  /* The signature is the pullquote’s attribution here, not a sign-off at the
     foot of a letter, so it sits directly under the quote. */
  .quote-attrib { margin-top: var(--space-3); font-size: var(--size-3); }
  .after-quote { margin-top: var(--space-6); }

  /* ── the boundary diagram ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .example { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-7); align-items: start; }
  .example-body { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-4); }

  .boundary {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    background: var(--surface-panel);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-raise);
    padding: var(--space-6);
    counter-reset: none;
  }
  .boundary li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 2px;
    padding: var(--space-3) 0;
  }
  .boundary .b-k {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-1);
    color: var(--text-quiet);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-mono);
  }
  .boundary b {
    font-size: var(--size-5);
    font-weight: var(--weight-strong);
    color: var(--text-loud);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
  }
  .boundary .b-n { font-size: var(--size-3); color: var(--text-quiet); line-height: var(--lh-snug); }

  /* The one item that is a question rather than a step, marked as the moment
     the responsibility changes. */
  .boundary .b-line {
    background: var(--accent-quiet);
    border-radius: var(--radius-inner);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    margin-block: var(--space-2);
  }
  .boundary .b-line b { color: var(--accent-ink-strong); }

  /* ── explores list ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .explores { max-width: 880px; }

  /* ── commitment ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .commit { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-7); align-items: start; }
  .commit-copy { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-4); }
  .commit-facts {
    background: var(--surface-panel);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-raise);
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-6);
  }
  .commit + .price-card { margin-top: var(--space-7); }

  /* ── forms ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Centred, not flush left: the form sits under a two-button chooser, and
     hanging it off the left edge reads as belonging to the left-hand button
     rather than to whichever one is selected. `auto` restores the centring
     global.css already gives .apply-form. */
  .forms :global(.apply-form) { margin-inline: auto; }

  /* The branch chooser. Two buttons, not a <select> — each needs room for a
     one-line description, and a visible pair is one fewer click than a
     dropdown for a decision this page exists to help someone make quickly. */
  .route-choice {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
  .route-choice-btn {
    text-align: left;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    background: var(--surface-panel);
    border: var(--border-w-hair) solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-raise);
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-6);
    cursor: pointer;
    font: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .route-choice-btn:hover { box-shadow: var(--shadow-lift); }
  .route-choice-btn:focus-visible {
    outline: var(--focus-w) solid var(--focus-ring);
    outline-offset: var(--focus-offset);
  }
  /* The selected branch is marked the same way the design system marks any
     selected control — ink, never sun; sun is a fill for an action, not a
     state, and this button is not "go", it is "chosen". */
  .route-choice-btn[aria-pressed='true'] { border-color: var(--text-loud); }
  .rc-title {
    font-size: var(--size-4);
    font-weight: var(--weight-strong);
    color: var(--text-loud);
  }
  .rc-body { font-size: var(--size-2); color: var(--text-quiet); line-height: var(--lh-snug); }

  .route-panel:focus { outline: none; }

  /* THE ARRIVAL CUE. Landing on #apply from an Apply CTA closes both forms
     and lights both choices, and the highlight HOLDS until somebody picks —
     the class stays on, so these declarations outlast the pulse. The form
     used to be open on arrival, which meant the chooser was the thing a
     reader scrolled past on their way to the fields, and the team route was
     invisible to anyone who did not look up. Border and ink only: --sun is a
     fill for an action, and a button being pointed at has not been chosen —
     which is also why this has to look different from the pressed state
     above, and does, by the shadow. */
  .route-choice-btn.is-cueing {
    border-color: var(--text-loud);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-lift);
    animation: rc-cue 500ms var(--ease-out) 3;
  }
  @keyframes rc-cue {
    0%,
    100% {
      border-color: var(--text-loud);
    }
    50% {
      border-color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
  }
  /* The cue still happens for somebody who has asked for less motion — it
     just holds the highlight instead of pulsing it. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .route-choice-btn.is-cueing { animation: none; }
  }

  .contact-line {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    font-size: var(--size-3);
    color: var(--text-quiet);
  }
  .contact-line a { color: var(--accent-ink); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 0.2em; }

  /* ── narrow ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .two-up,
    .example,
    .commit {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-5);
    }
    .band { padding-block: var(--space-6); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .commit-facts { padding-inline: var(--space-5); }
    /* One less full section of vertical padding per screen on a phone, where
       the extra room this token gives on a desktop reading distance is just
       more scrolling. */
    .band { padding-block: var(--space-5); }
    .route-choice { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-3); }
  }
</style>
